Part of the Perl CLI strand (see the strand card). Step 3: prove a binary on Linux. No workflow and no release on this card.

Pack: perl/bin/skilletor with PAR::Packer, the house way.
- Skill perl-par-binary-release (getty source), installed here via skilletor:
  - build-binary.sh and loaded-modules.pl: one computed module set for build and verify;
  - PAR_VERBATIM=1;
  - verify-binary.sh: a fresh PAR_GLOBAL_TEMP, env -i, the binary's output equal to perl -Ilib bin/skilletor on success and error paths;
  - check-binary-libs.sh: RUNTIME_LIBS and the glibc floor.
- Build with Perl 5.40 or newer, which Text::Nunjucks needs (k159).
- Text::Nunjucks is not on CPAN: it comes from the local checkout or a git clone.
- Modules loaded by name must be in the computed set: the Source backends, the Error classes, Text::Nunjucks internals.

Prove: run the parity harness, every `cli` case, against the packed binary inside a container with neither perl nor node.
- Image: debian:stable-slim plus git and ca-certificates, with perl-base removed. debian:stable-slim carries /usr/bin/perl through perl-base, which is Essential. The cell asserts that `command -v perl` and `command -v node` both fail.
- The temp dirs are bind-mounted at the same paths.
- SKILLETOR_CONFORMANCE_CLI points at a `docker exec` wrapper.

Measure and report on this card, against node dist/skilletor.js on the same host:
- the binary's size;
- cold start (first run, empty PAR cache) and warm start;
- three commands: `--version`, a no-op `hook session-start`, and `hook user-prompt-submit` (it runs at every prompt, 10 s timeout). `check` as a fourth.
- what could lower the start time: the module set, lazy loading per command, pp -z, the PAR cache location, …

