k188 result: measurements and recommendation.

Host: Debian 13, system perl 5.40.1 (shared libperl), PAR::Packer 1.064, node v24.19.0, 16 cores. Load average was 2.8 to 8.4 during the runs because other agents were busy. Harness: every command forked with an env built from nothing (PATH=/usr/bin:/bin, HOME, CODEX_HOME, LC_ALL=C), output to /dev/null, round robin over all variants each round (so load drift hits every variant), 2 warm-up runs. Cold = a fresh empty PAR_GLOBAL_TEMP per run; warm = one cache per binary. Three runs (21, 15, 25 rounds). Medians moved by up to 2x with load from one run to the next; minimums stayed within about 10%. So the cost is given as min (best of N, range over the runs), and the spread as median [p25-p75] from the quietest run (load 2.8-3.7). Fixture: user scope, one local source, 2 skills (one .njk), synced. On it check exits 1 "changed" for node and perl alike.

