Where warm --version time goes (v2, about 395 ms): PAR boot floor about 250 ms (63%: loader plus PAR, Archive::Zip and IO::Compress compiled on every start), the 345-entry archive about +45 ms, the CLI's modules about +110 ms (Report -> Frontmatter -> Error/Moo, Path::Tiny, Encode; ranked with a CORE::GLOBAL::require timer on the source tree). NYTProf cannot run inside the binary: the packed perl ignores PERL5OPT and PERL5LIB (@INC is the cache's inc/lib, inc and two PAR hooks). That is why the floor binaries exist. Side effect, a good one: an installed older Skilletor cannot leak into the binary.

What lowers start time, ranked by measured effect:
1. Leaving out the families (-X, in build-binary.sh): cold -2.35 s (3.2 s -> 0.8 s), warm --version -130 ms, warm status -140 ms, size -7.2 MB. Done.
2. Lazy loading in Skilletor::CLI past --version/--help (CLI.pm is k173's, not done here): warm --version -100 to -120 ms (down to the archive floor), cold -70 to -100 ms; status and check do not change. lazyB is only 15-25 ms better than lazyA, within the noise. Recommendation for k173: lazyA's shape (require the four Skilletor helpers after the --help check).
3. -z 0: cold -50 to -90 ms, warm unchanged, +6.4 MB. Not worth it.
4. -n noscan: -0.75 MB, start within noise. It makes loaded-modules.pl the only defence against a missing module. Not recommended.
5. -z 1 / -z 9: no measurable effect.
Not reducible inside PAR::Packer: about 250 ms warm for a script that does nothing. Warm, the binary is about 3x node for --version and about 4x for status/check. The first start after install or reboot is cold, 0.8-1.0 s; Debian 13's /tmp is tmpfs, so the PAR cache is gone after every reboot.

